  "query_preview": "..High pressure was centered over the California/Arizona\nborder early morning Thursday. In this position flow around the \nhigh will deliver drier air from the west to northwest. Limited \nmonsoon moisture will continue to linger along and south of the \nMogollon Rim and White Mountains with just slight chances for a \nstray shower or thunderstorm. Further north, the drier air will\nwin out resulting in fair and breezy conditions. Under high \npressure temperatures will remain on the hot side with mountain \nhighs ranging from the upper 70s to mid 80s and 95 to 105 at lower\nelevations.\n\nFor Friday and the weekend..A trough will dive across the Pacific\nNorthwest pushing the California/Arizona high center eastward. As\nthe shifting trough brushes Arizona the models continue to show  \ndecent coverage of showers and thunderstorms Friday and Friday \nnight. Vertical wind shear will strengthen some with the passing \ntrough which usually means a few storms will become organized with\nisolated severe thunderstorms and localized flash flooding \npossible. The trough moves eastward on Saturday and Sunday but \nmonsoon moisture will linger with at least slight chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms continuing. Under the influence of the \ntrough it will become breezy with general west to southwest winds \nat 10-20 mph gusting to 25-30 mph starting Friday and lasting \nthrough the weekend. Cooler with temperature dropping to slightly\nabove seasonal average by Sunday.\n\nLooking into next week...Troughing will continue over the Pacific \nNorthwest early next week with a series of weak shortwave troughs \nbrushing Arizona and interacting with monsoon moisture. While it \nis difficult to predict exactly how things will pan out it appears\nthat unsettled conditions will continue with at least slight \nchances for daily showers and thunderstorms. Models still showing \na stronger low diving southward off the west coast later next week \nturning the flow more southerly over Arizona resulting in perhaps\nbetter chances for showers and storms.",
